<div class="section" aria-labelledby="find-h">

Findings

## Five findings, strongest first

<div class="finding">

### 1. One office controls the county’s money — and holds a veto over its legislature.

The Executive proposes and signs the \$3.5B biennial budget and can veto
Council legislation; the Council appropriates it. Roughly 77% of the
General Fund goes to law enforcement and the courts, so the elected
Sheriff (\$406M) and Prosecuting Attorney (\$94.1M) sit at the center of
county spending even as they answer to voters, not the Executive. County
power concentrates in offices that cannot easily be overridden.
<sup>[\[E001, E004\]](#src-05)</sup>

</div>

<div class="finding">

### 2. All six major economic anchors rest on government-granted advantages.

Federal appropriations fund JBLM outright; tax exemption (plus
Medicare/Medicaid reimbursement) underlies MultiCare’s \$7.2B; federal
recognition and a state gaming compact underlie the Tribe’s casino
economy; Weyerhaeuser’s acreage traces to an 1864 land grant sold once,
cheaply, to a railroad; the Port is a public taxing entity; the Chamber
is tax-exempt. This is the finding that survived every hostile check,
entity by entity, with primary documents. <sup>[\[E007,
E009\]](#src-jblm)</sup>

</div>

<div class="finding">

### 3. The same few people sit on both sides of the table.

Three documented dual roles anchor the interlock pattern: the Chamber’s
CEO, Tom Pierson, was also appointed the county’s acting
economic-development director; MultiCare’s board chair, John Wiborg,
sits on the Chamber board and is a former GTCF board member; labor’s
Nathe Lawver chairs United Way of Pierce County while sitting on both
the GTCF and Economic Development Board. Ryan Mello himself is a former
GTCF board member now holding the executive’s office. These are
documented overlaps — the records show who shares tables, not what
happens at them. <sup>[\[E008, E010\]](#src-interlock)</sup>

</div>

<div class="finding">

### 4. Party money is lopsided; tribal money is bipartisan.

In the 2024 executive race, both finalists raised over half a million
dollars (\$509,801 for Mello; \$549,574 for Chambers). The Republican
money was concentrated — one \$175,500 check from the county party plus
\$28,500-class transfers from caucus committees; the Democratic money
was broader — unions (UFCW, IBEW 483, IAFF 726, WEA PAC), the HDCC
(\$28,500), and individual donors. The Puyallup Tribe gave to both
parties (\$2,400 to Mello among others) — documented donations from a
sovereign actor with land-use business before the same governments. The
report asserts the flows, not any exchange.
<sup>[\[E004\]](#src-pdc)</sup>

</div>

<div class="finding">

### 5. The county’s only daily newspaper is owned by an out-of-state hedge fund.

The News Tribune’s parent, McClatchy, is owned by Chatham Asset
Management (NJ) — acquired in bankruptcy in 2020 through a ~\$312M
credit bid plus \$49M cash. Its editor, Stephanie Pedersen, edits four
McClatchy papers at once; the AP wire was dropped in 2024; the county
has no locally owned TV station. Narrative capacity is real but
*extracted*: what gets covered locally is decided two time zones away.
<sup>[\[E006\]](#src-media)</sup>

</div>

</div>

