<div class="section" aria-labelledby="media-h">

Narrative power

## One daily paper, owned elsewhere

Ownership chain of the only daily: **Chatham Asset Management (NJ)** →
**McClatchy Media Company** → **The News Tribune** (Tacoma; founded
1880; locally owned by the Baker family for 73 years until McClatchy
bought it in 1986 for ~\$112M). Around it: three radio groups, one
business weekly, a weekly paper — and zero locally owned TV. The editor
edits four papers at once; the opinion editor was appointed in 2025. In
a county where the Port, the Tribe, MultiCare, and the Council all
generate daily coverage decisions, the outlet that frames them is not
locally owned. <sup>[\[E006\]](#src-media)</sup>

</div>

<div class="section" aria-labelledby="lim-h">

What this map cannot say

## Limits, open to the reader

<div class="limits-card">

- **The computed power score is a network measure, not a power
  measure.** Graph centrality over-counts connected hubs (parties, the
  Council, courts) and under-counts isolated veto-holders (the Tribe,
  JBLM, the Sheriff). That is why this report uses the qualitative
  matrix and tier bands instead of raw scores.
- **“Shadow political party” is a characterization, not a finding.** The
  Chamber’s Candidate Academy, Voters Guide, and Candidates Forum are
  documented; it officially does not endorse candidates. This report
  describes the programs and stops there.
- **The “growth machine” is an academic lens, not a proven
  organization.** Donor flows between developers, realtors, banks, and
  the Chamber are documented; coordinated direction is not.
- **Two numbers failed verification and appear nowhere above:** the
  MultiCare-linked “\$357K against Democrats in 2022” figure (actually
  attributed to the hospital association’s PAC, unconfirmed against PDC
  C4 reports) and the claim that the Tribe is the county’s *largest*
  lobbying spender (federal lobbying of \$270K in 2024 is verified; the
  superlative is not).
- **Corrections carried into this report:** JBLM’s \$12.1B is a
  two-county multiplier; the Tribe’s \$162M is a 1990 settlement, not
  current assets; the tribal chairmanship rotates annually; the council
  appropriates *to* the sheriff, courts, and prosecutor (edge directions
  were reversed in an earlier draft); Kastama sits on Puyallup’s city
  council, not the county council; T’wina Nobles sits on the University
  Place school board.
- **Not yet checked:** current council committee chairs for the 2026
  session; Swank’s early biography details; a Pierce-only authoritative
  MultiCare headcount.

</div>

