<div id="method" class="section" aria-labelledby="method-h">

Methodology

## How this was built, and verified

Researchers mapped 163 entities across six domains — government (53),
institutional (47), economic (32), political (17), media (8), legal (6)
— and recorded 342 relationships, each with a typed label (board seats,
funding, donations, lobbying, ownership) and a weight. Then the work was
attacked:

1.  **Hypercritical audit** (E008) — every node, edge, and ranking
    checked against the evidence files; 12 wrong edges, 12 filler edges,
    and 45 missing entities found and fixed.
2.  **Red-team review** (E009) — every claim assumed wrong until a
    primary source (.gov, WA PDC, IRS 990s, Fitch, court records)
    confirmed it; ~80% survived, and every mischaracterized figure was
    corrected.
3.  **Three skeptic subagents** (E010) — ranked the ranking, the
    omissions, and the money separately, catching the Council over-rank,
    Kelly Chambers’ inflated score, the buried Tacoma mayor, and the
    wrong Chamber budget (\$4.5M → \$3.08M).
4.  **Deep profiles** (E013–E014) — all 163 entities researched against
    primary sources, 43 new edges, deterministic consistency sync (272 →
    342 edge count), with per-entity confidence ratings.

Composite scores blend six domains; the interactive graph adds a
veto-power bonus for actors whose influence is concentrated and
non-negotiable (the Tribe, JBLM, the Sheriff, the Chamber) rather than
distributed. Full method: *evidence/26-power-methodology.md*.

</div>

